                "Next, the engine enters a 2-second Stationary Zero-Rate Calibration Window. It collects 500 contiguous sensor frames over SPI DMA. The calibration engine computes the sample variance across each axis: if variance exceeds $0.05 \\text{ dps}$, motion or vibration is detected, and the calibration restarts. If stationarity is verified, the engine calculates the mean zero-rate bias vector: $\\vec{b}_{gyro} = [b_x, b_y, b_z]^T$. These calibrated offsets are locked into non-volatile memory and automatically subtracted from all subsequent sensor frames: $\\vec{\\omega}_{cal} = \\vec{\\omega}_{raw} - \\vec{b}_{gyro}$.",
